// arc's lamp channel: one low-side lamp off the bus rail, and the divider a bank reads its rail
// through. Ported from arc's `shared::lamp`.
//
// What the port is testing:
//
// - **Values are stated; facts are derived; assertions hold facts to bounds.** The Rust block
//   solved its ballast from the rail's window, so a change two sheets away re-bought the resistor
//   and nothing said so. Here the ballast is a parameter with a chosen value, and what the rail
//   does to it is a set of facts the block asserts over: bind the channel to a rail it was not
//   sized for and the assertion names the resistor and the current, rather than the BOM changing.
// - **A port carries a fact the block is the only one to know.** The die is off-board, so the
//   block is what knows the cathode lead sits at the rail less the forward drop. `set net.voltage` on
//   the cathode port says so; the current is then a fact of that voltage and a named resistance.
// - **A load that states itself.** `add net.draw current` on the rail port, so a bank of twelve reads
//   as 45 mA on VPWR.
// - **A body stated at the placement.** 60 mW in a 1206 rated 250 mW; the project's 0402 would sit
//   at 95 % of its rating for as long as an aspect is lit. `footprint=` is how a generic part
//   takes a body the policy would not give it.

use "@stackup/passives"
use "@stackup/discrete/fet/dmn3404l"

// A common-anode string: VPWR → the LED at the signal → its cathode lead → the ballast on the
// board → the switch → GND. Run from the rail rather than the regulator, so the heat of standing
// 16 V above a 2 V die lands in a resistor built for it, and the switch is a sink — an MCU still in
// reset shows no aspect, which for a railway signal is the safe one.
block lamp {
    // Chosen, not solved. 4.3 kΩ is (18 V − 2 V) / 4 mA rounded up to E24, and the assertions
    // below are what hold that arithmetic.
    param ballast resistance default="4.3kΩ"
    // 2.0 V, the smallest drop a signal die has (red, yellow, AlInGaP green), so the ballast's
    // dissipation is its worst case; an InGaN green or a white at ~3 V just runs a milliamp dimmer.
    param forward voltage default="2.0V"
    // What a lamp may draw at the top of the rail — bright in a gallery, a tenth of what a 0603 die
    // is rated for, and where firmware dims from.
    param limit current default="4mA"

    // The bus rail the lamp's common anode sits on. The channel draws its peak from it.
    port rail type=power {
        add net.draw current
    }
    // PWM in — high lights the lamp, and duty is brightness.
    port gate type=pwm
    // The lamp's cathode lead, ahead of the ballast. When the lamp is lit it sits at the rail
    // less the die's drop, with the drain at ground under it. Not named here: the bank that
    // places the header a lead lands on is the one with a word for it.
    port cathode {
        set net.voltage "rail.voltage - forward"
    }

    derive current "cathode.voltage.max / ballast"
    derive dim "cathode.voltage.min / ballast"
    derive watts "cathode.voltage.max * current"
    text sizing "lamp ballast — {current} at {rail.voltage.max}, {dim} at {rail.voltage.min}; {watts} at the top of the rail in a 1206 rated 250 mW"

    assert "current <= limit" \
        message="{ballast} lets {current} through at {rail.voltage.max}; the lamp is sized for {limit}"
    assert "watts <= 125mW" \
        message="the ballast dissipates {watts} at the top of the rail, over half of a 1206's 250 mW"
    assert "dim >= 1mA" \
        message="at {rail.voltage.min} the lamp draws only {dim}, and firmware cannot raise a duty past 100 %"

    // A DMN3404L — logic-level, on at 2.5 V of gate drive where a BSS138 is only just.
    place dmn3404l switch note="low-side switch — sinks the lamp's cathode through the ballast"
    circuit switch.source rail.gnd

    // The gate: the MCU's timer channel drives it, and the pull-down is what it rests on when
    // nothing does. It is the one part here that exists for a failure rather than a function:
    // with the MCU in reset its pins are high-impedance, and a gate left to float can lift enough
    // on leakage to light a lamp nobody commanded.
    place pull-down gate-pulldown node=gate rail=rail value="100kΩ" \
        note="an undriven gate is a dark lamp — an MCU in reset shows no aspect"
    circuit gate switch.gate

    // Drain, ballast, cathode. The drain is named because it is where a meter goes to find out
    // whether a dark lamp is a dead switch or a broken lead.
    place resistor R value=ballast intent=series footprint="Resistor_SMD:R_1206_3216Metric" note=sizing
    circuit switch.drain R cathode
    circuit switch.drain name="drain"
}

// The rail-sense divider a lamp bank reads its rail through: VPWR × 0.1525 for an ADC pin —
// 100 kΩ over 18 kΩ is 2.75 V at 18 V, 1.75 V at the floor, and still under 3.3 V with both legs at
// the wrong end of 1 %. A 100 nF across the tap gives the converter's sample-and-hold something
// stiff to charge from behind 15 kΩ of source. What firmware does with it: the duty each lamp
// runs at is (V_nominal − V_f) / (V_measured − V_f) scaled to the aspect's brightness, so a signal
// looks the same on a 13 V leg as on a 16 V one.
//
// The same rule as the lamp: the legs are chosen, the tap's voltage is a fact of them and the
// rail, and what the tap must stay under is asserted rather than solved for.
block rail-sense {
    param high resistance default="100kΩ"
    param low resistance default="18kΩ"
    // What the converter reading the tap can take — the MCU's rail, less a margin for both legs
    // at the wrong end of 1 %.
    param ceiling voltage default="3.0V"

    port rail type=power
    port tap type=analog {
        set net.voltage "rail.voltage * ratio"
    }

    derive ratio "low / (high + low)"
    text reading "VPWR × {ratio} for an ADC pin — {tap.voltage.max} at the top of the rail"
    assert "tap.voltage.max <= ceiling" \
        message="the tap reaches {tap.voltage.max} at {rail.voltage.max}; the converter takes {ceiling}"

    place resistor Rtop value=high intent=divider note=reading
    place resistor Rbot value=low intent=divider
    place filter Cfilter node=tap rail=rail value="100nF" \
        note="holds the tap stiff for the converter's sample-and-hold"
    circuit rail.rail Rtop tap Rbot rail.gnd
}
